I have a MacBook Pro 7.1 (the latest model currently available) and
installed Ubuntu 10.10 x64 from the posted ISO on the website.=A0 By
default, only a single processor was utilized by the OS until I added
the line:

acpi_apic_instance=3D2

to the bootloader as suggested in kernel logs.

With this simple addition, both cores were detected.=A0 This is an email
to simply state that the suggestion did in fact work.=A0 Attached is the
output to this command:

cat /var/log/messages | grep "log source" -m 1 -A 300 | tee bootlog.txt


Here is the kernel version (same as in the subject): 2.6.35-23-generic
#40-Ubuntu SMP Wed Nov 17 22:14:33 UTC 2010 x86_64 GNU/Linux

Let me know if any more information would be useful to you.

